--- title Asking what a value is
--- about The questions that answer a logic. Most are obvious; the ones
--- about pinned here are the ones where the obvious answer is wrong,
--- about and the boundaries that separate two questions that look like
--- about the same one.

--- id predicates/truth-of-a-number
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
true? 1
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/zero-is-true
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The single most surprising thing about the language. Only NONE
--- note and logic FALSE are false.
--- code
true? 0
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/an-empty-string-is-true
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
true? ""
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/an-empty-block-is-true
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
true? []
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/nothing-is-false
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
true? none
--- result
#(false)

--- id predicates/false-is-false
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
true? false
--- result
#(false)

--- id predicates/did-asks-the-same-question
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note DID is TRUE? under a name that reads better in front of a call.
--- code
did 1
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/did-of-nothing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
did none
--- result
#(false)

--- id predicates/a-whole-number-is-a-number
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
number? 1
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/a-decimal-is-a-number
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
number? 1.0
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/money-counts-as-a-number
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which the standard NUMBER! typeset does not say. The predicate
--- note is wider than the typeset it is named after.
--- code
number? $1
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/a-string-of-digits-is-not-a-number
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
number? "1"
--- result
#(false)

--- id predicates/equivalence-ignores-the-datatype
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note So EQUIV? is the loose comparison, not the strict one its name
--- note suggests. STRICT-EQUAL? is the one that minds the datatype.
--- code
equiv? 1 1.0
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/equivalence-ignores-case
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
equiv? "a" "A"
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/the-negation-of-equivalence
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
not-equiv? 1 1.0
--- result
#(false)

--- id predicates/plain-text-is-ascii
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
ascii? "abc"
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/an-accent-is-not-ascii
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
ascii? "aé"
--- result
#(false)

--- id predicates/nothing-at-all-is-ascii
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The degenerate case answers true rather than false, which is
--- note the useful way round for a guard.
--- code
ascii? ""
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/a-character-can-be-asked-too
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
ascii? #"a"
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/an-accent-fits-latin-one
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The next range up, so this is the boundary between the two
--- note questions.
--- code
latin1? "aé"
--- result
#(true)

--- id predicates/well-formed-bytes-have-no-bad-position
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note INVALID-UTF? answers where the trouble is rather than whether
--- note there is any, so none means well-formed.
--- code
invalid-utf? #{41}
--- result
_

--- id predicates/malformed-bytes-answer-where
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold invalid-utf? #{FF}
--- result
"#{FF}"

--- id predicates/the-sixth-item
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3 4 5 6]
sixth b
--- result
6

--- id predicates/the-sixth-item-of-a-shorter-series
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
sixth b
--- result
_

--- id predicates/taking-the-first-item-and-stepping-on
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note FIRST+ answers the first item and leaves the series one along,
--- note which is the whole difference from FIRST.
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
first+ b
--- result
1

--- id predicates/where-the-series-is-left-afterwards
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: [1 2 3]
first+ b
mold b
--- result
"[2 3]"
